    <!-- one line per recent version (newest first), "version|summary";
         the About screen parses the pipe — keep entries single-line -->
    <string-array name="about_changelog">
    <item>1.4.9|Dropping a tab group beside another group lands exactly beside it (the invisible \"teleport to the bottom\" corpse: a stale position memory sent drops to the wrong end of the list, and the settle wrote the wrong side); the top-right menu is grouped into labelled sections; every tab inside a tab group and every shortcut inside a home folder gets its own visible \"\u22ef\" menu — edit name and link, de-group, delete — no hold gesture needed. Under the hood: the adblock engine stops compiling ~130,000 regex Patterns (a ~130 MB memory corpse — plain-literal rules now ride one compact Aho-Corasick scan per request, only rules that truly need regex keep a Pattern), a dead write-only WebView registry that pinned every closed tab\'s context in memory is deleted, and the tab list no longer races the background media service (the ConcurrentModificationException corpse).</item>
        <item>1.4.8|Empty tab groups settle exactly where you leave them (a neighbour-anchored position memory replaces the shifting row index) and tabs can finally sit right beside an empty group; the shortcut flow asks for the folder only after you choose Add to browser home; folders grow from their own window and edit sheet (a + tile and an Add shortcut row); site settings are chunked into labelled groups.</item>
        <item>1.4.7|Only the tab group you actually hold moves (empty groups stop travelling with their neighbours — and drag themselves at last); background playback survives rotating the phone to landscape; the add-shortcut dialog can file a site straight into a home folder.</item>
        <item>1.4.6|The home folder window\'s icons finally drag (a listener-eating corpse under every gate); empty tab groups keep their top spot when switching to follow-tab-order; every delete in the app — tab, shortcut, folder, bookmark, saved page, watch-later — gets a 3.5-second Undo.</item>
        <item>1.4.5|Switching groups-on-top to follow-tab-order keeps the layout exactly where it was (then drag anything anywhere); the in-folder tab drag and the folder-window icon drag actually work on device (a scroll-stealing corpse); tabs and folders no longer vanish on the switch (atomic saves, no stale cross-process rewrites, duplicate-folder heal).</item>
        <item>1.4.4|The hidden game paces itself (floatier jumps, slower bars, wider gaps); tabs rearrange freely beside folders in follow-order and inside the folder rows; folder-window icons rearrange by dragging; saved passwords fill every account of a site (with a picker) and two-step sign-ins remember the username.</item>
        <item>1.4.3|The hidden game survives a failure (tap to retry works); switching group placements never moves your folders; password saving actually writes the vault (and autofill finally has something to fill).</item>
        <item>1.4.2|The hidden game actually launches (a spawn-loop hang froze it on a black screen); empty folders no longer vanish in follow-tab-order; About lives in deep settings; bookmarks and shortcuts can rank above history while typing.</item>
        <item>1.4.1|The folder window lays its icons out as a proper matrix; About gains this changelog and a hidden game; one tab order across both group placements.</item>
        <item>1.4.0|The launcher folder window, the draggable tab groups, the disappearing-folder fix and this About screen with the update checker.</item>
        <item>1.3.100|The grouping dwell takes a full second to fill; sign-in-with-GitHub round-trips ride one stable identity (the qwen connect fix).</item>
        <item>1.3.99|The version agreement: the UA and the client-hints claim the same Chrome version (the grok gate).</item>
        <item>1.3.98|The home drag vs. page-swipe clash fixed; the desktop site survives back-navigation.</item>
        <item>1.3.97|No more phantom home refresh on early drags; the icon menu is quicker and fully iconified.</item>
        <item>1.3.96|The home-drag crash fixed; save-AI-response and the whole-page PDF work.</item>
        <item>1.3.95|The new-tab crash fixed; the search-history fill button; the unhurried home menu.</item>
        <item>1.3.94|The home-screen crash loop fixed; the honest backup import; the ask-first dark pipeline.</item>
    </string-array>

